One of the focused PRs that replace #24643. Independent of the others; can land in any order.

## Problem

The deferred column sync worker ticked once a minute and skipped blocks less than five slots old. When gossip missed some custody columns, the block stayed unavailable for over a minute. For Gloas this also held back the execution payload envelope that waits for the block's data, so the FULL variant and the head lagged behind.

## Changes (`cl/das/peer_das.go`)

- The worker ticks every second, which bounds how late a due root is picked up. A queued root becomes due after a grace counted from the later of its slot start and the moment it was queued. A custody node needs every custody column to see its data available when it attests, so gossip gets half the time to the attestation deadline and the by-root fetch the other half: 2 s before Gloas, 1.5 s from Gloas on (mainnet). Archive nodes wait a full slot, since they recover from half the columns.
- Download rounds run off the ticker, each bounded at a slot, with a per-root in-flight marker. A root nobody serves does not stall the others. The queued blocks and their retry state are kept in one map under one mutex.
- A failed round grows the root's backoff by one slot, up to an epoch. At the cap, a root is given up only when the beacon indices name another block at its slot, or no block where the indexed chain skipped the slot. A slot above the indexed head is undecided and the root is kept: the index covers the head chain only. The check runs with the worker's context, since the round's own context has expired when a round leaves columns missing. On a custody node, a storage error waits a slot without counting as an attempt; on an archive node the availability helpers log a storage error and report the data unavailable, so it counts as one.
- Roots whose slot left the data-column serve range are dropped. Without peers the availability scan is skipped; a failed peer count is logged at Warn, no peers at Trace.

The re-apply of a parked envelope once its data is complete is not part of this PR: on main, `RetryDataAvailablePendingExecutionPayloadEnvelopes` already polls data-available parked envelopes from `SleepForSlot`.

Known limits are tracked in issues:
- custody backfill for forward-synced blocks: #24704;
- the queue keeps the full block rather than its recovery metadata: #24789.

## Tests

`cl/das/deferred_column_sync_test.go`:
- the due time, the grace counted from enqueue, and the grace per node type and fork;
- the capped backoff;
- a storage error is not an attempt;
- a root dropped while in a round stays dropped after `failed`, `retryLater` and the due scan;
- the canonical check against a real index DB: other root, skipped slot, slot above the head, missing index, read error;
- the worker drops roots outside the serve range and roots already available;
- the worker skips the availability scan without peers;
- the worker gives up an orphan after a round that timed out (archive mode, a peer that serves nothing, a backoff cap of one slot, an index that names another root).

The canonical-head rule and the timed-out round were red first. Mutation-checked: the read-error and dropped-root branches, `forget`, the in-flight check, the halved grace, the peerless skip, the first enqueue time kept, and the canonical check with an expired context.

`go test -race` passes for `cl/das/...` and `cl/phase1/network/services`; `make lint` is clean twice.

